  /* Unit 2 · Lovernance: an overview of three kinds of land (cards linking to #lens-governance/2-1 etc.), then 2.1 Needs-meeting commons, 2.2 Collective-owned homes, 2.3 Give-and-get program. Law rows start with a word (Helps, In the way, County by county, Federal, Not yet law), never colour alone. */
  var UNIT2 = "<section class=\"gv-sec\"><p>All start in the same place: the minimum isn’t what rent costs, it’s what it costs to meet needs directly. Rain caught on a tarp, seeds from a seed library and cuttings from a neighbor cost close to nothing (<a href=\"#lens-water/sources\">Water: Sources</a>). The one thing that takes money is land, so that’s where governance comes in.</p><div class=\"gv-visions\"><article class=\"gv-vision\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">2.1 · Public</span><h3>Needs-meeting commons</h3><p class=\"gv-vshort\">The campus: shared houses, farmed land and a station for the block, open to anyone.</p><a href=\"#lens-governance/2-1\">Read 2.1 ↓</a></article><article class=\"gv-vision\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">2.2 · Owned together</span><h3>Collective-owned homes</h3><p class=\"gv-vshort\">A private satellite: feels like your own house, sized to who lives there.</p><a href=\"#lens-governance/2-2\">Read 2.2 ↓</a></article><article class=\"gv-vision\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">2.3 · Private</span><h3>Give-and-get program</h3><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Homes people own that stay private and share something real.</p><a href=\"#lens-governance/2-3\">Read 2.3 ↓</a></article><article class=\"gv-vision\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">2.4 · Renting</span><h3>Renters</h3><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Renters go first at the commons, with more ways to stay and to own.</p><a href=\"#lens-governance/2-4\">Read 2.4 ↓</a></article><article class=\"gv-vision\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">2.5 · People and animals</span><h3>Shelters to sanctuaries</h3><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Free places to exist without harm. No animal is killed on lovernance land.</p><a href=\"#lens-governance/2-5\">Read 2.5 ↓</a></article><article class=\"gv-vision\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">2.6 · Sovereign land</span><h3>Tribal lands</h3><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Tribal law stays the tribe’s, with the same safety and the first invitation to steward.</p><a href=\"#lens-governance/2-6\">Read 2.6 ↓</a></article><article class=\"gv-vision\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">2.7 · Backyards, storefronts, offices</span><h3>Room we already have</h3><p class=\"gv-vshort\">ADUs, a storefront given as a home, and empty office floors turned into commons dorms.</p><a href=\"#lens-governance/2-7\">Read 2.7 ↓</a></article><article class=\"gv-vision\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">2.8 · Living land</span><h3>Trees and wild neighbors</h3><p class=\"gv-vshort\">No tree cut and no animal killed without a just cause. Plant more trees.</p><a href=\"#lens-governance/2-8\">Read 2.8 ↓</a></article></div></section><section class=\"gv-sec\" id=\"gv-2-1\"><div class=\"gv-sec-head\"><span class=\"gv-n\">2.1</span><div><h2>Needs-meeting commons</h2><p>Public</p></div></div><p class=\"gv-vshort\">One fallback that isn’t a punishment: a good place to live where your needs are met.</p><p>Anyone can walk in, especially people with nowhere else to go. Most commons won’t be built from scratch; they’ll be conversions. The house next door becomes a commons, and the whole block gains a station for getting needs met. Picture a campus that specializes in meeting needs: shared houses, farmed land, and a music night at the hall down the block. Want a different life? You’re free to try your luck elsewhere.</p><h4>How needs get met</h4><ul><li><b>Day one:</b> a bed, water and food. Rest comes first, and nobody is asked to work at the door.</li><li><b>Always there to fall back on.</b> After a divorce, a lost job, leaving your parents’ home or any fresh start, there’s a room in shared housing.</li><li><b>Clothes and clean.</b> A starter wardrobe when you arrive, laundry and soap, then a yearly gift of clothes and linens, delivered like a birthday present. Tailoring and repairs count too, because looking good is a need (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-2\">3.2</a>).</li><li><b>A station for the block:</b> water refills, showers, toilets, laundry, a pantry, thrift, repair and a tool library, open to neighbors.</li><li><b>Food for residents first,</b> then outward to neighbors.</li><li><b>Play and gather:</b> sports fields, courts, pools, stages, dance and music halls, and game rooms, open to everyone.</li><li><b>A public materials store:</b> a thrift run by the commons, with lumber, fabric, tiles and salvage for anyone building or mending, and cheap hardware at the tool library.</li><li><b>Foster animals.</b> Shelter dogs, cats and rabbits can live with residents who want them, with food, vet care and backup care provided.</li><li><b>Work that meets real needs.</b> Worker co-ops are like unions that own the shop, and existing unions are friends (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-9\">3.9</a>): a plumber meets real needs too. The direction is “I’m on call in the neighborhood to fix your pipe, and my family has a home,” not price-gouging for executives.</li><li><b>Worker co-ops</b> make what people need: beds, natural-fiber clothing, tools, solar, internet and seed.</li><li><b>One price for everyone.</b> When there isn’t enough, need comes first and residents get a set discount.</li><li><b>One at a time:</b> a bed, not 75 beds to resell. Trade in an old model toward a new one, and the old one gets repaired or passed on.</li><li><b>Not a tragedy.</b> Commons last when the people who use them make the rules, with small responses first and conflicts worked through close to home (Elinor Ostrom; <a href=\"#lens-relationships/dialogue\">Dialogue</a>).</li><li><b>The land</b> is held by a community land trust. Members give time instead of money. <b>Rentals can become co-ops</b> that residents own, at real cost.</li><li><b>Anyone can leave,</b> with their skills and credit for what they gave.</li></ul><h4>California today</h4><ul class=\"gv-law\"><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span><i>City of Santa Barbara v. Adamson</i> (1980): the state right to privacy struck down a cap on unrelated people sharing a house.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> Assessors must count a land trust’s resale limits, keeping property tax lower (§402.1).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> The Williamson Act (1965) taxes contracted farmland as a farm. The Rainwater Capture Act (2012) lets you use roof rain without a water right.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> The Worker Cooperative Act (2015), plus SB 1079 (2020) and San Francisco’s COPA (2019), which give tenants, land trusts and co-ops a chance to buy when certain homes sell.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> In a declared shelter crisis, a city or county can swap state building and zoning rules for simpler local ones, but only for shelters on public land (Gov. Code §8698.4, until 2036).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">County by county</span> Class K lets people build simpler rural homes, only where adopted. Shelter and safe-parking rules vary too.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> State policy is that no adoptable animal be euthanized if it can be adopted, and shelters already run foster programs (the Hayden Law, 1998).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> Rural zoning, contractor-grade building codes, land prices, and, since <i>Grants Pass</i> (2024), citations for sleeping outside.</li></ul></section><section class=\"gv-sec\" id=\"gv-2-2\"><div class=\"gv-sec-head\"><span class=\"gv-n\">2.2</span><div><h2>Collective-owned homes</h2><p>Owned together · All in</p></div></div><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Homes given to the program, held together and shared by match.</p><p>Dense or spread out. People who live at the commons have collective-owned homes too, just high-density ones. A single suburban house given in is a low-density one. Whoever lives there joins give-and-get: the backyard might hold a vegetable bed or the block’s water filter, meeting needs for the neighborhood and for them. Ready to move? Give your whole home, and get a comparable one. That’s how land moves from private control into shared hands: one home at a time, by choice. Going all in rezones the land for meeting needs, for good.</p><h4>What it feels like</h4><ul><li><b>A private home.</b> Nobody wanders in. Care comes by appointment: “Your farmer comes Thursdays at 11,” a mold check, a plumber for the broken pipe.</li><li><b>Sized to who lives there.</b> A studio fits one person or a couple. A five-bedroom fits five: a family plus housemates, or friends.</li><li><b>Big houses, full lives.</b> An empty mansion becomes a famous group house, like young people living in the Haight-Ashbury homes where music history happened.</li><li><b>A satellite of the commons.</b> It meets needs the way the commons does, at home scale, with the commons always there as backup.</li></ul><h4>How needs get met</h4><ul><li><b>Two ways to give.</b> A <b>kin gift:</b> the home becomes collective-owned, and each home you give covers one household for good, your kids first or anyone you name, chosen family included. Or a <b>trade:</b> no sentimental tie, so you take your standing somewhere new and the home opens to the neighborhood. Either way, you can keep living in your home for life, and it goes to the program after.</li><li><b>Pledge now, kids safe now.</b> A pledge of your home is a recorded contract (a life estate, with the home passing to the land trust), not a promise anyone can quietly take back. In return, your kids or the household you name get standing today: a commons room, or a spare room or studio in a collective-owned home. “Go to the commons” hands them safety instead of a mortgage carried alone.</li><li><b>Standing moves with you.</b> Going all in earns standing: a lasting promise of a home anywhere in California. Moving frees your home for someone else, so moving is welcome.</li><li><b>Inherit a house you don’t want?</b> Trade it. Once homes aren’t tied to jobs, Humboldt’s forests are open too.</li><li><b>Rank homes honestly.</b> The match is built so honesty is your best move, and nobody ends up with a home they like less than the one they gave. Homes people want most simply get more requests (top trading cycles, the idea behind kidney exchange).</li><li><b>Children first.</b> Families with kids get first pick of private homes, and the match keeps kids in their school district (<a href=\"#lens-governance/4-1\">4.1 Least able first</a>).</li><li><b>Matched like college housing:</b> for how long, who must live with you, and whether you’re open to housemates.</li></ul><h4>When a home opens</h4><p>Everyone gets a home eventually. Until there are enough, this is who is fully covered first.</p><ol><li><b>Kin first rights:</b> one household for each home given, the giver’s kids first or whoever they name.</li><li><b>Someone already living there,</b> like the last resident’s grown kid.</li><li><b>Neighbors’ nominees,</b> closest neighbors first. Nominees meet the same need standard as everyone else.</li><li><b>Past residents</b> who marked themselves “open to offers.” Anyone marked “happy where I am” is never contacted.</li><li><b>The waitlist,</b> least able first (<a href=\"#lens-governance/4-1\">4.1</a>).</li></ol><p><b>Nearest first, need decides.</b> The same rule runs for homes, shops and spaces (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-4\">3.4</a>).</p><h4>Homes owned by companies</h4><ul><li>Companies are people underneath, often pension funds for teachers and firefighters. Their needs are steady income and a clean way out.</li><li><b>Sell to the program</b> at an appraised price paid over years: steady income, with the tax spread out too.</li><li><b>Renters get first chance</b> to buy their homes as co-ops, and property managers can stay on as paid stewards.</li><li><b>Sell for less, and the difference counts as a gift.</b></li></ul><h4>Asked by size</h4><p>The more homes an owner holds, the bigger the share asked to meet needs.</p><ul><li><b>Mom-and-pop (1 to 4 homes):</b> say yes to tenants’ gardens, compost or a hosted room, and get free land testing, repairs from NMB crews and tenants who stay.</li><li><b>Small (5 to 49):</b> some homes at the town’s fair rate, for tax relief and shared maintenance.</li><li><b>Large (50 or more):</b> a share meeting needs that grows each year, for faster permits and public contracts.</li><li><b>Institutional and private equity (hundreds or more):</b> sell to land trusts at a fair price over years, or convert a set share. 400 homes is 400 households.</li></ul><h4>California today</h4><ul class=\"gv-law\"><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Federal</span> The ROAD to Housing Act (2026): from January 7, 2027, for-profit owners of 350 or more single-family homes can’t buy more, with exceptions like build-to-rent. It doesn’t force sales, so a way out matters.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Federal</span> McKinney-Vento: students without stable housing can stay in their school of origin, with transportation.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Federal</span> Selling over time spreads the tax across years (installment sales, §453). Giving your home while keeping the right to live in it for life can count as a charitable gift (§170).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> A deed can give your home to a land trust while you keep the right to live there for life (a life estate), so the gift is fixed and passes without probate.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> A home trade is a change in ownership, which resets property tax to today’s value.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Not yet law</span> Zoning for given homes, portable standing, and a statewide match. Doors for advocacy (<a href=\"#lens-governance/5.3\">5.3 Stairs for change</a>).</li></ul></section><section class=\"gv-sec\" id=\"gv-2-3\"><div class=\"gv-sec-head\"><span class=\"gv-n\">2.3</span><div><h2>Give-and-get program</h2><p>Private · Step in</p></div></div><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Your home stays yours. Opt in, and your land gets lighter rules for meeting needs.</p><p>Your land is your slice of heaven for as long as you live there and look after it, and it can pass to whoever you choose. Your zoning stays the same: joining is an agreement with the program, not a rezoning, so it’s the transition step and easy to step back from (going all in is what rezones, 2.2). It’s the step-in model, like joining a grocery co-op: get involved and benefit without going all in. <b>It’s invite-only:</b> you decide who stays, like a close friend in a tough spot. Open it to people you don’t know, after a meeting where either of you can say no, and you get the most support of all. Give in <b>ongoing gifts</b> or <b>one-time gifts</b>. Everyday gifts stay informal. A promise of land or a home is written down as a contract, so everyone can count on it.</p><h4>Ways to give</h4><ul><li><b>Grow.</b> Gardeners grow food with you. You keep enough for your household, and the abundance goes to the commons. Start with one bed, and ask for watering help anytime.</li><li><b>Water and make.</b> Room for rain tanks, a filter station or a maker’s bench, with first use for you.</li><li><b>Care.</b> Dog walks, babysitting swaps, help for someone with a disability, meals after a birth or a loss.</li><li><b>Shelter.</b> Someone you invite sleeps in their car, a tent or a cabin. They owe you nothing. Host someone you don’t know, and you get a support worker, a mediator and the home-care fund behind you.</li><li><b>Host a caretaker.</b> Hire a caretaker from a needs-meeting business to tend your garden, animals or home, and they live in your spare room. An empty bedroom houses someone, with the commons as their backup.</li><li><b>Bridge homes.</b> “My kids want it in five years.” Someone lives there until then, knowing it ends, with a commons home guaranteed next.</li><li><b>Foster.</b> Take in a shelter animal for a while, with food, vet care and backup care from the commons.</li><li><b>Lend it to friends.</b> Away every Christmas? Let a friend’s family stay. Nobody tracks it; a friend’s home instead of a hotel is already the gift.</li><li><b>Never-empty pledge.</b> Your home won’t sit unused for more than a year. Nobody takes it from you.</li></ul><h4>Shelter without a $500k ADU</h4><ul><li>A guest needs a safe place to sleep and a bathroom. A car, tent or small cabin works with <b>bathroom access</b> (yours, a commons station nearby, or a serviced portable or certified composting toilet), <b>smoke and CO alarms</b>, a fire extinguisher and a clear way out.</li><li><b>Want a real backyard home?</b> State law now lets most single-family lots add ADUs (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-7\">2.7</a>).</li></ul><h4>Safety for givers</h4><ul><li><b>A state-backed home-care fund</b> repairs damage. Damage means someone is struggling, so it’s met with cleaning help and care, not fines.</li><li><b>One check-in a year</b> on the house’s needs, by a neutral town steward or by you. Neighbors can flag the land, not the people.</li><li><b>You choose how much the home can change:</b> keep it as is, decorate and restore, or make it yours. Gardens, ramps and rain tanks stay.</li></ul><h4>What you get</h4><ul><li><b>The safety membership</b> and the co-op shop (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-2\">3.2</a>), and lighter rules on your land for meeting needs.</li><li><b>Housing someone counts most.</b> A guest or caretaker in an empty bedroom is someone newly housed. A promise for later isn’t yet, so it earns membership and your household’s standing (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-2\">2.2</a>).</li></ul><h4>Thanks, not rewards</h4><ul><li><b>Basics are never earned,</b> and everyday care isn’t counted. What gets written down is coordination, not credit (Alfie Kohn).</li><li><b>Safety without hoarding.</b> When health care, food and a home are reliably shared, hoarding has less and less to do.</li></ul><h4>California today</h4><ul class=\"gv-law\"><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> Prop 13 (1978) caps property tax at 1% of the last sale price, so the longer someone holds, the less they pay, even for an empty home.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> Building codes treat anywhere people sleep as a home, and most places require a flush toilet. The shelter-crisis shortcut covers only public land.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> The Ellis Act stops cities from forcing owners to keep renting (and in August 2026 an appeals court tentatively said it preempts San Francisco’s empty-homes tax), so pledges stay voluntary.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> Anything a guest gives for a stay can look like rent, making them a tenant with rules a car or tent can’t meet.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> Owner move-in is a just cause to end a tenancy (SB 567): family moves in within 90 days and stays a year. That fits bridge homes.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> Owners who let people garden are shielded from most injury suits (Civil Code §846), unless they get something in return. Food donors are liable only for gross negligence (AB 1219).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> Some counties already pay landlords for damage when they house people leaving homelessness (Marin: up to $3,500). In-Home Supportive Services pays caregivers, family included.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Not yet law</span> A give-and-get permit, with no rezoning, carrying emergency-housing safety rules (like Appendix X of the state residential code) on private land, a hosted-shelter category and a home-care fund in every town.</li></ul></section><section class=\"gv-sec\" id=\"gv-2-4\"><div class=\"gv-sec-head\"><span class=\"gv-n\">2.4</span><div><h2>Renters</h2><p>Renting</p></div></div><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Renters go first at the commons, with more ways to stay and to own.</p><p>Most people who rent don’t have land to give, so they get first access to what the commons offers. Landlords are welcome too; renters just go first. And renting can be a stable, good life, with real ways to stay put or own your home together.</p><h4>How needs get met</h4><ul><li><b>Renters first at the commons.</b> When garden beds, commons homes or spots in the match are limited, people without land go first.</li><li><b>Keep your rent going while you can.</b> The commons is a fallback while it grows, not a reason to stop. Meanwhile, start on the NMB side (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-1\">3.1</a>), give the time you can, and talk with your landlord about turning your unit over (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-2\">2.2</a>).</li><li><b>Grow where you rent.</b> Pots on a balcony, patio, stoop or windowsill are yours to plant, and rooftops with your landlord’s OK. Gardeners can come help (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-3\">2.3 Grow</a>). Fire escapes stay clear, as fire code requires.</li><li><b>A lawyer if you face eviction,</b> free, everywhere in California, not only in San Francisco.</li><li><b>First chance to buy.</b> When your building sells, renters, with a land trust behind them, get the first offer and can turn it into a co-op (2.1).</li><li><b>Renters first for needs-meeting goods and jobs.</b> When goods or openings at needs-meeting businesses are limited, renters go before landlords (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-1\">3.1</a>).</li><li><b>The same door for landlords.</b> A landlord who gives their extra homes to become collective-owned gets the same access. Everyone gets the same chance; where you stand with land only changes your first step, and nobody has to leave real safety to take it. It’s moving from betting on the market to betting on something fairer.</li><li><b>Join give-and-get with your landlord’s OK:</b> a shared garden bed, compost for the block, a rain tank. <b>Landlords who say yes</b> get free land testing, repairs from NMB crews and tenants who stay.</li><li><b>A landlord’s no never holds you back.</b> Work, volunteer shifts and crafts count the same, and renting never limits your jobs or your place in line.</li><li><b>The path home:</b> a commons room first, then a collective-owned home sized to your household (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-2\">2.2</a>).</li><li><b>Landlords have needs too:</b> steady income and a clean way out. Selling to the program over years meets both (2.2).</li></ul><h4>California today</h4><ul class=\"gv-law\"><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> The Tenant Protection Act (AB 1482, 2019) caps most rent increases at 5% plus inflation, never more than 10% a year, and requires a just cause to end most tenancies after a year.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> San Francisco’s Prop F (2018) gives every tenant facing eviction a free lawyer. 55.6% of voters said yes.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> The Neighborhood Food Act (AB 2561, 2014) lets renters grow food in portable containers.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> SB 1079 (2020) and San Francisco’s COPA (2019) give tenants, land trusts and co-ops a chance to buy when certain homes sell.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> Costa-Hawkins (1995) blocks rent control on single-family homes and on newer buildings. Votes to repeal it failed in 2018, 2020 and 2024.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Not yet law</span> A statewide right to a lawyer in eviction, a statewide first chance to buy, and renters-first access to commons land.</li></ul></section><p class=\"gv-src\">Sources: <i>City of Santa Barbara v. Adamson</i>, 27 Cal.3d 123 (1980); Cal. Rev. &amp; Tax. Code §402.1; Williamson Act (1965); Rainwater Capture Act of 2012; Cal. Code Regs. tit. 25 (Class K); Cal. Gov. Code §8698.4; Cal. Const. art. XIII A (Prop 13); SB 1079 (2020); S.F. Admin. Code ch. 41B (COPA); AB 816, California Worker Cooperative Act (2015); Cal. Gov. Code §7060 (Ellis Act); SB 567 (2023); Cal. Civ. Code §846; AB 1219 (2017); Marin County Landlord Partnership Program; 21st Century ROAD to Housing Act (2026), Title X; McKinney-Vento Homeless Assistance Act, 42 U.S.C. §11431 et seq.; 26 U.S.C. §§170(f)(3), 453; Gale and Shapley (1962); Shapley and Scarf (1974); AB 1482, Tenant Protection Act (2019); S.F. Proposition F (2018); AB 2561, Neighborhood Food Act (2014); Costa-Hawkins Rental Housing Act (1995); Alfie Kohn, <i>Punished by Rewards</i> (1993); Elinor Ostrom, <i>Governing the Commons</i> (1990).</p>";

  /* Units 2–3 sections use sec2 and dashed ids. S25 (2.5 Shelters to sanctuaries), S26 (2.6 Tribal lands), S27 (2.7 Room we already have) and S28 (2.8 Trees and wild neighbors) belong to unit 1. */
  function law(rows) { return '<h4>California today</h4><ul class="gv-law">' + rows.map(function (r) { return '<li><span class="gv-tag">' + r[0] + '</span> ' + r[1] + '</li>'; }).join('') + '</ul>'; }
  function sec2(n, title, kicker, short, body) {
    return '<section class="gv-sec" id="gv-' + n.replace('.', '-') + '"><div class="gv-sec-head"><span class="gv-n">' + n + '</span><div><h2>' + title + '</h2><p>' + kicker + '</p></div></div>' + (short ? '<p class="gv-vshort">' + short + '</p>' : '') + body + '</section>';
  }
  function go(n, label) { return '<a href="#lens-governance/' + n.replace(/^(\d)\.(\d+)$/, function (m, u, k) { return +u < 5 ? u + '-' + k : m; }) + '">' + (label || n) + '</a>'; }
  var S25 =
    sec2('2.5', 'Shelters to sanctuaries', 'People and animals', 'Free places to exist without harm. Stay a night, or stay for good.',
      '<p>A shelter today is often a waiting room with a clock on it. A sanctuary is a place to exist without harm, with no deadline, for people and for animals. Lovernance land is nonviolent land: nobody is turned away for having nowhere to go, and no animal is killed there.</p>' +
      '<h4>For everyone who arrives</h4>' + ul([
        '<b>Stay a night, or stay for good.</b> Some people land here for a while before finding a home that fits. Others stay and end up caring for whoever comes next. Either way, it can be home.',
        '<b>Caretaking is give-and-get.</b> Feeding, cleaning, walking dogs and sitting with someone new all count as time given (' + go('2.3') + ').',
        '<b>Not a cage.</b> Doors open both ways. Nobody is held, and nobody is moved on for lack of space. Trouble is met with care (' + go('4.5') + ').']) +
      '<h4>Animals</h4>' + ul([
        '<b>No killing on lovernance land.</b> When land joins, it promises nonviolence: no slaughter, no hunting, no fishing and no poison. Fish in backyard ponds are welcome.',
        '<b>Schools and town backyards too.</b> Schools are commons, so no animal is killed on school land, and the same goes for backyards in town. Nobody is sent off-site to do it instead: the commons doesn’t pay for slaughter anywhere.',
        '<b>Farmers can step out a barn at a time,</b> starting with crops like mushrooms (' + go('3.1') + ').',
        '<b>No euthanasia for space or “behavior.”</b> Fear and biting usually come from trauma in a scary place. Time, room and patient people help.',
        '<b>Adopted out through the commons.</b> Farmed animals and animals retired from labs go to sanctuaries or to people who want them, with neighbors helping care for them.',
        '<b>Fostering is a give-and-get gift.</b> Shelter animals can live with anyone in the commons who wants them, with food, vet care and backup care (' + go('2.1') + ').',
        '<b>Rescued farm animals, cared for all their lives.</b> Hens and other animals bred to grow or lay too fast often live in chronic pain. They get vet care and as much peace as possible, the way anyone with a disability would. Their surplus eggs are shared as gifts, never sold (' + go('3.1') + ').',
        '<b>Born into peace.</b> Sanctuary animals aren’t bred for products, so every animal born from here on is born into a world of peace.',
        '<b>Kids meet them.</b> Schools have a sanctuary to visit, so children get to know cows, goats and hens as someone, not something (' + go('4.2') + ').',
        '<b>Sheep instead of mowers.</b> Picture a quiet city-block backyard with the fences down, gone wild again and grazed by a small flock. It starts when neighbors agree, and as homes become collective-owned, the last fences come down in their own time.',
        '<b>Kinder pest care.</b> Prevention first: netting, sealed gaps and companion plants. Leave a sacrificial bush for the caterpillars and gophers, and move or shoo animals rather than kill them.',
        '<b>Cats and wildlife.</b> What a cat catches and eats is between the cat and the wild; nobody polices predators. A cat that kills for sport without eating stays supervised or in a catio, to protect the wildlife the commons lives among. Cats already living outside are cared for where they live (' + go('2.8') + '). People don’t kill animals on-site to feed pets. Vegan pet food is made by an NMB, and anyone can still buy their own (' + go('3.1') + ').',
        '<b>Not about your plate.</b> People eat what they choose, and nobody checks anyone’s lunch, a burger from down the street included. The rules are for the land and for needs-meeting businesses, not for people.',
        '<b>Why it matters:</b> a slaughterhouse next to homes is loud, unsafe and hard to live beside, and moving it out of sight solves nothing. The work is among the most dangerous there is: fast lines, knives and repeated strain. Farmers who raise animals under contract often borrow heavily to build barns to a company’s specs. A sanctuary meets needs for animals and people at once.']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'California bans animal cruelty (Penal Code §597) and the smallest cages for some farm animals (Prop 12, 2018).'],
        ['Helps', 'Labs must offer dogs and cats they no longer need for adoption through shelters and rescue groups, rather than euthanize them (AB 147, 2015).'],
        ['Helps', 'San Francisco puts prevention first and limits pesticides on city property (Integrated Pest Management Ordinance, 1996).'],
        ['In the way', 'Meat and poultry plant workers face fast line speeds, sharp tools and chemicals, and their injuries are likely undercounted (GAO, 2016).'],
        ['County by county', 'San Francisco lets a household keep two goats without a permit; more, or other hoofed animals like sheep, take a permit (Health Code §27).'],
        ['Helps', 'A promise can run with the land: deed covenants bind later owners (Civil Code §1468), and conservation easements protect habitat for good (Civil Code §815).'],
        ['Not yet law', 'Nonviolent land as its own zoning category, sanctuary rules for grazing animals in cities, and no euthanasia for space in any shelter.']]) + '<p class="gv-src">Sources: Cal. Penal Code §597; Proposition 12 (2018); AB 147 (2015); S.F. Environment Code ch. 3 (Integrated Pest Management, 1996); S.F. Health Code §27; GAO-16-337, meat and poultry worker safety (2016); Cal. Civ. Code §§815, 1468; Hayden Law (SB 1785, 1998).</p>');
  var S26 =
    sec2('2.6', 'Tribal lands', 'Sovereign land', 'Tribal land stays under tribal law, with the same safety and the first invitation to steward.',
      '<p>California is Native land. Tribes are sovereign nations, and their land isn’t the state’s to rezone. So tribal land is the one place lovernance doesn’t start with zoning. It joins the way a pledge does: by agreement, nation to commons, on the tribe’s own terms.</p>' +
      ul([
        '<b>The same safety.</b> Tribal members get the same membership, standing and commons homes as everyone, anywhere in California, whether or not their tribe joins.',
        '<b>Never rezoned.</b> Lovernance doesn’t ask for tribal land. A tribe can make an agreement with the commons for whatever land or work it chooses.',
        '<b>Peace land is a promise any land can make.</b> Where a tribe keeps hunting or fishing, as is its right, that land partners through give-and-get, the same rule as for any land (' + go('2.3') + ').',
        '<b>First invitation to steward.</b> Tribes are invited first to tend commons land, as paid gardeners, land stewards and keepers of fire and water, and to teach how this land was cared for long before.',
        '<b>Land back counts.</b> Returning land to a tribe counts as a gift, the same as giving it to the commons.',
        '<b>Named with respect.</b> Each commons learns and names the people whose land it’s on.']) +
      law([
        ['Federal', 'Tribal trust land is governed by the tribe and federal law. California’s criminal jurisdiction there (Public Law 280, 1953) doesn’t give counties zoning power (<i>Santa Rosa Band of Indians v. Kings County</i>, 1975).'],
        ['Helps', 'In 2019 California’s Governor apologized for the state’s violence against Native peoples and created a Truth and Healing Council (Executive Order N-15-19).'],
        ['Helps', 'A 2020 state policy encourages agencies to return ancestral land to tribes and support tribal access, co-management and stewardship.'],
        ['Helps', 'The Esselen Tribe regained 1,199 acres near Big Sur in 2020. In the East Bay, the Sogorea Te’ Land Trust, led by Lisjan Ohlone women, returns land, and neighbors pay a voluntary Shuumi land tax.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'Stewardship agreements between tribes and commons, and land-back gifts counted the same as gifts to the commons.']]) +
      '<p class="gv-src">Sources: Public Law 83-280 (1953); <i>Santa Rosa Band of Indians v. Kings County</i>, 532 F.2d 655 (9th Cir. 1975); California Executive Order N-15-19 (2019); Statement of Administration Policy on Native American Ancestral Lands (Sept. 25, 2020); Western Rivers Conservancy, Esselen Tribe land return (2020); Sogorea Te’ Land Trust.</p>');
  var S27 = "<section class=\"gv-sec\" id=\"gv-2-7\"><div class=\"gv-sec-head\"><span class=\"gv-n\">2.7</span><div><h2>Room we already have</h2><p>Backyards, storefronts and offices</p></div></div><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Backyards, empty storefronts and empty office floors can hold homes now.</p><p>Most new homes don’t need new land. They fit in backyards, behind empty shop windows and on empty office floors. For owners, this can be a way out: a storefront nobody will rent, or a tower that won’t fill again, stops being a loss and starts housing people. Letting go of a bet that isn’t paying is good news for everyone involved.</p><h4>Backyard homes (ADUs)</h4><ul><li><b>Room for more.</b> California now lets most single-family lots add a backyard cottage, a unit attached to the house or made from a garage, and a junior unit inside the house. Cities still add their own limits, so check yours.</li><li><b>Build it as give-and-get.</b> An ADU that houses a caretaker, a bridge-home family or a neighbor counts as housing someone (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-3\">2.3</a>). NMB builders and the commons materials store keep costs down (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-1\">2.1</a>, <a href=\"#lens-governance/3-3\">3.3</a>).</li><li><b>Start smaller if you need to.</b> Not every guest needs a $500,000 cottage. A small cabin with a bathroom nearby and working alarms can be a start (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-3\">2.3 Shelter</a>).</li><li><b>For your kids, or for anyone.</b> An ADU can be a bridge home, or part of a pledge that gives your household standing now (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-2\">2.2</a>).</li></ul><h4>Storefronts</h4><ul><li><b>A storefront counts as a home given.</b> It’s a full space: a room, often a sink and a bathroom, and a door to the street. Give one to the program and it counts the same as a house, covering one household for good (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-2\">2.2</a>).</li><li><b>Live, work, or both.</b> It can become a home, an NMB with a home in back, or a shop for the block (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-4\">3.4</a>).</li><li><b>A clean way out.</b> An owner paying a loan on an empty storefront can sell it to the program over years, or give it, and stop the loss.</li><li><b>A step in the right direction.</b> San Francisco’s Vacant to Vibrant (2023) gave pop-up shops three months of free rent in empty downtown storefronts. Lovernance keeps the space in use for good.</li></ul><h4>Offices</h4><ul><li><b>If you can work there all day, you can live there.</b> An office good enough for 9 to 5 is good enough to sleep, cook and work from home in, once it has real rooms. Today the main thing in the way is the label on the building.</li><li><b>Offices as commons.</b> Empty floors downtown are already shared space. Add walls and doors that lock, and a floor becomes a commons dorm: private rooms, big shared bathrooms and showers, and a kitchen, the way a college dorm works (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-1\">2.1</a>).</li><li><b>Walls, not curtains.</b> A home needs real privacy and safety, so every room gets walls and a door that locks. A curtained cot can be a night off the street, never a home.</li><li><b>The messy parts, said out loud.</b> Deep floors put windows far from the middle, many windows don’t open, and bathrooms sit in one core. Those make full apartments expensive. A dorm fits better: rooms along the windows, shared space in the middle, and fresh air from the building’s own system.</li><li><b>Downtown comes alive.</b> People who want to live downtown can, and the shops below have customers after 5.</li><li><b>Owners get a way out</b> of towers that won’t fill again: a sale to the program over years, or a gift (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-2\">2.2</a>).</li></ul><h4>California today</h4><ul class=\"gv-law\"><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> Cities must approve backyard homes on single-family lots without a hearing: a detached ADU, an attached or converted one, and a junior ADU inside the house. Since 2026, they must also review applications on a deadline and charge smaller units lower fees (Gov. Code §66323; SB 543, 2025).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">County by county</span> Cities still set details like size, height and setbacks, and some write rules the state later strikes down. Check your city’s ADU page.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> Homes can be built by right on land zoned for offices and shops when the project meets affordability and labor standards (AB 2011, 2022).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> AB 507 (2025) takes a fast track San Francisco tried first (AB 2488, 2024) statewide: office-to-housing conversions are approved by right when at least half the space becomes homes.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> San Francisco voters waived the transfer tax on the first sale of a converted office building, up to 5 million square feet citywide, for projects approved by 2029 (Prop C, March 2024).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Elsewhere</span> Vacant to Vibrant (San Francisco, 2023) gave pop-ups three months of free rent in empty storefronts, plus grants of up to $8,000.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> Offices are built and permitted for business. Living there takes a change of use, and housing codes ask for light, fresh air, a safe way out and enough bathrooms for everyone.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Not yet law</span> A storefront or office floor counted as a home given, office dorms as commons, and live-work permits for any empty commercial space.</li></ul><p class=\"gv-src\">Sources: Cal. Gov. Code §66323; SB 543 (2025); AB 2011, Affordable Housing and High Road Jobs Act (2022); AB 2488 (2024); AB 507 (2025); S.F. Proposition C (March 2024); S.F. Office of Economic and Workforce Development, Vacant to Vibrant (2023).</p></section>";
  var S28 = "<section class=\"gv-sec\" id=\"gv-2-8\"><div class=\"gv-sec-head\"><span class=\"gv-n\">2.8</span><div><h2>Trees and wild neighbors</h2><p>Living land</p></div></div><p class=\"gv-vshort\">No tree cut and no animal killed without a serious, just cause. And plant more trees.</p><p>Trees and wild animals are neighbors too. Lovernance treats cutting a tree the way tenant law treats ending a tenancy: it takes a just cause.</p><h4>Trees</h4><ul><li><b>A serious, just cause.</b> Meeting a real need counts: food, or a home where there’s nowhere else to build. Wanting a view, fewer leaves or a parking spot doesn’t.</li><li><b>Groves grown for use.</b> Woodlots planted for lumber, firewood or poles are meant to be cut. Harvesting healthy wood there meets a need, and the grove is replanted.</li><li><b>Some trees belong to everyone.</b> The tree in the town square, the old street tree, the one a school grew up under: what they give the whole town outweighs one household’s need, so they stay.</li><li><b>Danger, checked by an arborist.</b> A dead or failing tree over a home can come down, after pruning and thinning are tried. Fire clearance follows fire law, done as gently as it allows.</li><li><b>A tree spared is a gift.</b> A landlord or owner who planned to cut trees and doesn’t is giving in give-and-get (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-3\">2.3</a>).</li><li><b>Plant more.</b> Every commons plants trees: shade for hot blocks, fruit and nuts, native oaks and homes for birds. Tree planters and arborists are needs-meeting work (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-3\">3.3</a>), and anyone can ask for a street tree.</li></ul><h4>Wild neighbors</h4><ul><li><b>Nobody is killed for existing.</b> A mountain lion passing through, a coyote at night, a hawk over the hens: lovernance land keeps everyone safe by keeping animals apart, not by killing.</li><li><b>Protect instead.</b> Covered runs for hens, animals in at night, sturdy enclosures, motion lights and guard animals. The commons helps with the cost.</li><li><b>No poison.</b> Rat poison moves up the food chain into the hawks, owls and mountain lions that would have eaten the rats for free (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-5\">2.5</a>).</li></ul><h4>Cats outside, and what everyone leaves behind</h4><ul><li><b>Cats already living outside are cared for where they live:</b> food, water, shelter and vet care, by neighbors who sign up, which counts as give-and-get (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-3\">2.3</a>). The commons is their safe place too.</li><li><b>Public litterboxes.</b> Covered sand spots at commons and parks, scooped every day, give outdoor cats somewhere to go that isn’t a garden bed or a kids’ sandbox.</li><li><b>Pick up after any dog.</b> Bag stations everywhere, and picking up what someone else missed is a small public gift. Pet waste goes to its own compost, never to food gardens.</li><li><b>Birds count too.</b> A cat that kills for sport stays supervised or in a catio (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-5\">2.5</a>).</li></ul><h4>California today</h4><ul class=\"gv-law\"><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Federal</span> The Migratory Bird Treaty Act (1918) protects hawks, owls and most native birds. Killing one takes a federal depredation permit from the US Fish and Wildlife Service; USDA Wildlife Services often does the work.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> California also protects birds of prey and their nests (Fish and Game Code §3503.5).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> Mountain lions can’t be hunted for sport (Prop 117, 1990). In February 2026, lions of the Southern and Central Coast were listed as threatened under the state endangered species law.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> The state still issues permits to kill a lion that keeps killing livestock, though nonlethal steps must come first.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> California restricts the strongest rat poisons, which kill the hawks, owls and lions that eat poisoned rats (AB 1788, 2020; AB 2552, 2024).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">County by county</span> Tree protection is local. San Francisco protects street trees, “significant” trees near the street and named landmark trees, and removing one takes a permit (Public Works Code Article 16). Many cities protect native oaks.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> Cutting someone else’s tree without permission can cost double or triple the damage (Civil Code §3346).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> In high fire hazard areas, homes need cleared space around them (Public Resources Code §4291). Thinning and limbing often meet it without taking every tree.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> A 2022 law set a goal of 10% more city tree canopy than in 2018 by 2035 (AB 2251). CAL FIRE released a draft urban forest plan in 2026.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">County by county</span> San Francisco requires people to pick up after their dogs (Health Code §40).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Not yet law</span> A just cause to cut any tree, public litterboxes, and wildlife-safe land with no lethal removal.</li></ul><p class=\"gv-src\">Sources: Migratory Bird Treaty Act, 16 U.S.C. §§703–712; Cal. Fish &amp; Game Code §3503.5; Proposition 117 (1990); California Fish and Game Commission, mountain lion listing (2026); AB 1788 (2020); AB 2552 (2024); S.F. Public Works Code art. 16 (Urban Forestry Ordinance); Cal. Civ. Code §3346; Cal. Pub. Res. Code §4291; AB 2251 (2022); S.F. Health Code §40.</p></section>";
  var U3SEC = [['3.1', 'Needs-meeting businesses'], ['3.2', 'Membership'], ['3.3', 'Work that needs doing'], ['3.4', 'Shops and spaces'], ['3.5', 'Public jobs, kept'], ['3.6', 'Taxes, time and gifts'], ['3.7', 'Old debts'], ['3.8', 'Less to throw away'], ['3.9', 'Unions'], ['3.10', 'Clean power']];
  var U4SEC = [['4.1', 'Least able first'], ['4.2', 'Born into safety'], ['4.3', 'Health together'], ['4.4', 'Safer together'], ['4.5', 'Community safety'], ['4.6', 'Who decides'], ['4.7', 'Needs met, on your word'], ['4.8', 'Held at the end']];
  function sublist(list) { return '<ol class="gv-u2list">' + list.map(function (s) { return '<li><a href="#lens-governance/' + s[0].replace('.', '-') + '"><span class="gv-n">' + s[0] + '</span>' + s[1] + '</a></li>'; }).join('') + '</ol>'; }
  /* Unit 3 · Jobs and economy (dashed ids gv-3-1 … gv-3-10) */
  var UNIT3 =
    '<section class="gv-sec"><p>Unit 2 was housing, starting from where you live. This is the work and money those places share. It’s opt-in, and it grows a little at a time.</p>' +
    '<p>When needs are reliably met, there’s less to steal, fight or police over. If a 24/7 diner a few blocks away will always feed you, nobody needs to break in for food.</p>' +
    sublist(U3SEC) + '</section>' +

    sec2('3.1', 'Needs-meeting businesses', 'All in or step in', 'Any business that meets real needs can go all in, or step in first with a fair-rate contract.',
      '<p>A needs-meeting business (NMB) sells what it makes, like any shop. What’s different is where the money goes. It can be a construction crew, a dental office, or a few people knitting socks together in someone’s living room while their coworkers work from home.</p>' +
      '<h4>Four musts to go all in</h4>' + ul([
        '<b>Fair shares.</b> Profits are shared by time and effort, about the same per hour for every role. Building a home is a team effort: the designer, the contractor and the person framing walls bring apples-and-oranges abilities, and each hour counts about the same. No profit leaves to outside owners or executives.',
        '<b>Elected leaders.</b> Workers choose the people who coordinate them, and can choose again.',
        '<b>Public pay.</b> The town posts every NMB’s pay for anyone to see.',
        '<b>Harms no animals.</b> No killing, and nothing newly killed is sold: no meat, no fish, no dairy, and no new leather or fur. Food is plant-based, plus gifts from sanctuary animals.']) +
      '<h4>Any legal shape</h4><p>A worker co-op fits best, because its paperwork already says the workers own it. But what counts is how a business works, not its forms. A nonprofit, an LLC, a partnership, an independent artist, even a classic corporation that meets all four musts in practice is an <b>honorary NMB</b>, with the same support. Nobody has to restructure to be welcomed in, and anyone can become a co-op on paper whenever they’re ready.</p>' +
      '<h4>Public servants, paid in money</h4><p>The community gives NMBs homes, spaces and gardens, so they’re public servants in spirit: paid in money, cared for by the people, working for the people. If prices look high and someone earns more than <b>twice the town’s median hourly pay</b>, a restorative practitioner invites them to talk it through with the community at a town meeting. It’s a conversation, not a fine.</p>' +
      '<h4>Enough, plus fun</h4><p>Shares are planned so everyone’s costs are covered, with money left over for fun. Living is more than getting by. The community credit union helps each business plan for that, unless housing and food are truly free, which is the hope.</p>' +
      '<h4>Plant-based, plus gifts from sanctuary animals</h4>' + ul([
        '<b>An invitation, not a ban.</b> Any restaurant can serve meat. It just isn’t an NMB, so it doesn’t get free space, homes or commons produce. Places that don’t need slaughterhouses make slaughterhouses unneeded.',
        '<b>Nobody polices customers.</b> Bring a burger to the commons cafe and nobody says a word (' + go('2.5') + ').',
        '<b>No dairy.</b> A mother’s milk is for her baby. If someone’s cow has milk to spare, it can be shared, never sold.',
        '<b>Free, or nearly, for members.</b> NMBs get free rent and safety, so they give freely in return. Members and give-and-get neighbors eat free or at a steep discount, counted by the meal, not by the ingredient. Eldercare counts the same as bringing the apples.',
        '<b>Money is welcome too.</b> Anyone can walk in and pay, living in the old economy for now. Money buys a meal made from plants, never animal products.',
        '<b>Eggs are gifts, never sold.</b> Nobody can buy them, so they’re never the cheap option. Surplus eggs from sanctuary hens go to members first, then to any neighbor when there are plenty, like any surplus. Egg dishes show up when there are eggs, never as a standing menu item.',
        '<b>Rare things run out.</b> Pomegranates don’t cost more than apples. When they’re gone, they’re gone, and a few go a long way across many salads.',
        '<b>Wool is a gift too.</b> Sanctuary sheep need shearing anyway. Knitters and weavers sell what they make, not the fleece.',
        '<b>Honey from backyard hives,</b> with bees unharmed: no killing queens and no culling hives for winter. Big apiaries crowd out native pollinators, so honey stays small and local, and wild bees get habitat and the same protection.',
        '<b>Secondhand, never new.</b> Existing leather, wool and fur can be worn, mended and passed on, never bought in, so no new trade grows.',
        '<b>Maple and local sweeteners.</b> Maple syrup makers get extra support, by trade from where maples grow, along with date syrup, fruit syrups and backyard honey closer to home.',
        '<b>B12 in the basics.</b> Plant-based eating needs a reliable B12 source, so fortified foods and supplements are part of membership (' + go('3.2') + ').',
        '<b>Vegan pet food is an NMB:</b> complete, vet-formulated food for cats and dogs, with checkups. Anyone can still buy their own pet food.',
        '<b>No poison.</b> NMB farms and gardens use no pesticides, herbicides or poison bait (' + go('2.5') + ').']) +
      '<h4>Ways in</h4>' + ul([
        '<b>Invited first.</b> The commons reaches out to businesses already doing this work: “We’d love to back you, if you go all in.”',
        '<b>Step in with a fair-rate contract.</b> Not ready to go all in? Meet a need at a rate the town publishes each year.',
        '<b>Started by the town.</b> When no business meets a need, the town hires people to meet it directly. That work grows into its own NMB, guided by an <b>NMB business advisor</b>, a new public role.']) +
      '<h4>Transfarmation: out a barn at a time</h4><p>Farmers who raise animals for a company often carry big loans and little say. The way out is a side business that goes all in while the rest of the farm steps out slowly.</p>' + ul([
        '<b>For the farmers, not the contract.</b> The farm family starts an NMB of its own on part of the land. The company’s contract money doesn’t make it one.',
        '<b>Start with high-value crops.</b> Mushrooms grow well in old barns, and sprouts, microgreens, herbs and berries pay well in small spaces.',
        '<b>No animal leaves to be killed.</b> The rest of the farm steps in through give-and-get (' + go('2.3') + ') only once it stops sending animals to slaughter: no restocking, and no animal sold, sent, or given to be killed, on the farm or off it. Farms raising animals the company owns step in after the last contract flock or herd leaves, and can ask the company to release animals to sanctuary.',
        '<b>Animals already there retire.</b> Animals the family owns live out their lives on the farm or at sanctuary (' + go('2.5') + '), and the commons helps with feed and vet care.',
        '<b>The commons buys first.</b> NMB kitchens and member stores are the first customers, so the new crop has a market on day one.']) +
      '<h4>Everyone’s invited, even the big ones</h4><p>Banks, hospitals and grocery chains meet needs too. Any of them can step in, or go all in by the same four musts as a corner bakery.</p>' + ul([
        '<b>Hospitals</b> can step in by caring at fair rates, forgiving medical debt and backing commons clinics (' + go('4.3') + ').',
        '<b>Lenders</b> can forgive debt, which meets a need as surely as a meal (' + go('3.7') + ').',
        '<b>Grocery chains</b> have the furthest to go, mostly because of meat. They can bring one store all in at a time, selling no animal products, while the rest of the chain steps in.',
        '<b>Something back.</b> Businesses that step in get fair-rate contracts, commons customers and commons deposits, and membership for their workers.']) +
      '<h4>What NMBs get</h4>' + ul([
        '<b>Priority for homes</b> for people who keep meeting community needs, part-time included, and parents taking time off to raise kids. Nobody loses a home for taking someone in, or because someone in the household works a private job.',
        '<b>A shared health program</b> on top of regular insurance (' + go('4.3') + ').',
        '<b>A four-day week,</b> and <b>work from home by default.</b> Workers set their own hours, as long as the work is done when it’s needed.',
        '<b>Restorative justice from outside.</b> When conflict happens, trained practitioners from outside the business help everyone repair it (<a href="#lens-relationships/dialogue">Dialogue</a>).',
        '<b>Gardeners and garden educators</b> for the land they use, and <b>first pick of commons spaces</b> (' + go('3.4') + ').']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'The Worker Cooperative Act (AB 816, 2015): a worker co-op can share its surplus by hours worked or by pay (Corp. Code §12243), worker-members elect the board, and outside “community investors” can’t run it.'],
        ['Federal', 'Co-op surplus paid out to members (patronage dividends) is taxed once, to the member, not twice like corporate profits (Subchapter T, 26 U.S.C. §§1381–1388).'],
        ['Helps', 'Since 2023, California job postings must show a pay range (SB 1162, 2022). Public employees’ pay is already public.'],
        ['In the way', 'Restaurants must use food from approved sources, so sanctuary flocks giving eggs to NMB kitchens would likely need to register with the state, as small egg producers do.'],
        ['Elsewhere', 'New York City has funded worker co-op development since 2014, a model for NMB business advisors.'],
        ['Helps', 'Other business forms can share power too: an LLC’s operating agreement can split profit by hours and let members elect managers, and a nonprofit can make its workers members who elect the board.'],
        ['Elsewhere', 'The Transfarmation Project (2019) helps farmers who raised chickens and pigs turn their barns to mushrooms, hemp and other crops. A hog barn in Iowa now grows mushrooms.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'NMB status, posted pay for every member business, fair-rate contracts, NMB business advisors, a harms-no-animals standard, honorary NMB status for any business form, and support for transfarming.']])) +

    sec2('3.2', 'Membership', 'Safety for everyone', 'Anyone can join the safety membership. What members buy keeps the system running.',
      '<p>People with money often hold on to it because they’re looking for safety. The membership offers the real thing, the same for everyone: a home tonight if disaster hits, health together and community safety. Members’ purchases help pay for it.</p>' +
      ul([
        '<b>Basics by need first.</b> Commons residents get a starter wardrobe, laundry, soap and a yearly gift of clothes and linens (' + go('2.1') + ').',
        '<b>Members buy healthy basics</b> at a rate the town sets each year: natural-fiber clothes, bedding, soap, B12 and food from commons farms, once people with the least have what they need.',
        '<b>Local makers get paid.</b> Purchases go to dyers, designers, seamstresses and repairers (' + go('3.3') + '), so healthy clothes can be fun too.',
        '<b>Equal access.</b> Membership is the same for everyone. Money buys goods, not a better place in line.',
        '<b>A way out of the rat race.</b> When the good life is easy to reach, contributing at an easy pace, like a weekly shift at a neighborhood restaurant, can be enough.']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'California’s consumer co-op law (Corp. Code §12200 and after) lets members own a store together and share its surplus. REI is one.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'A statewide safety membership with set-rate goods from needs-meeting businesses.']])) +

    sec2('3.3', 'Work that needs doing', 'Needs-meeting jobs', 'Start from rights and work backward to the jobs that make them real.',
      '<p>Water and a decent home are rights on paper (<a href="#lens-governance/5.1">5.1</a>). Making them real takes people. All of these can be NMBs, and get the most support when they are.</p>' +
      ul([
        '<b>Water and power:</b> plumbers, well and rain-tank installers, water testers, electricians, and the people who make and install solar panels, batteries and heat pumps (' + go('3.10') + '). <b>Public utilities</b> (water, power, gas, sewer, garbage, internet, transit) support everyone. When a private company runs one, the town treats it as an outside business it pays, free to go all in.',
        '<b>Building and infrastructure:</b> builders, roofers, electricians, natural builders, and road, bridge and transit crews.',
        '<b>Homes:</b> home cleaners, home restorers, furniture and bed makers, movers.',
        '<b>Land:</b> land restoration crews, soil and pollution experts and testers, toxicologists (<a href="#lens-toxins">Poisons</a>), tree planters and arborists (' + go('2.8') + ').',
        '<b>Food:</b> growers, garden educators, seed savers, compost and worm farms, food preservers (canning, drying, fermenting, shared freezers), bakers, cooks, grocers, and public kitchens, including a 24/7 diner within walking distance.',
        '<b>Health:</b> doctors, dentists who offer yearly exams, nutritionists, community health workers, herbalists, midwives, therapists.',
        '<b>Care:</b> childcare, elder care, disability care, hospice helpers and death doulas, pet care, veterinarians, animal sanctuaries and fosters.',
        '<b>Things:</b> seamstresses, cobblers, clothing and electronics repair, thrift, a tool library that sells cheap hardware, auto mechanics who offer yearly checkups, bike shops.',
        '<b>Waste:</b> composters, refill shops, and a plastics NMB (' + go('3.8') + ').',
        '<b>Place:</b> litter crews, artists and muralists, because a beautiful block is a need too.',
        '<b>Beauty:</b> dyers, designers and artists who make natural-fiber clothes fun, and seamstresses who tailor and mend, because looking good is a need too.',
        '<b>Learning, play and discovery:</b> skill educators, coaches, musicians, performers, and scientists, with research funded and results open to all.',
        '<b>Holding it together:</b> community credit unions (with a student debt fund), mediators and restorative practitioners, estate planners, home stewards, housing matchmakers, business matchmakers and NMB business advisors.']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'Public and co-op utilities already work: Sacramento’s public power (SMUD), rural electric co-ops, and community choice energy like CleanPowerSF.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'A needs-meeting jobs list tied to rights, with commons support for the businesses doing them.']])) +

    sec2('3.4', 'Shops and spaces', 'Nearest first', 'Empty storefronts come back when space goes to whoever shows up with care.',
      ul([
        '<b>A public registry of unused spaces:</b> every empty storefront, kitchen and workshop, open for anyone to see.',
        '<b>Nearest first.</b> The people who live closest get the first claim on a space, then the rest of the neighborhood. Need breaks ties. The same rule runs for homes (' + go('2.2') + ').',
        '<b>Say what you want to open.</b> “I’m a baker and my friend’s a chef, and we’d like a restaurant down the block.” Nearby businesses hear about it and are asked if they’d share space or join in.',
        '<b>Pitch to who’s already there.</b> When someone else wants a restaurant, they pitch it to the existing one, which may welcome the help and the vision.',
        '<b>A neighborhood business council.</b> Anyone can come to the meetings. Residents who attend vote, and residents who can’t are asked by email or text.',
        '<b>Fewer rules to start.</b> With the commons always there as a safety net, working for yourself needs less protection, so it can need less paperwork. People work because they want money for fun, gifts or taxes, not out of fear.',
        '<b>A storefront counts as a home.</b> Give an empty storefront to the program and it counts the same as giving a house (' + go('2.7') + ').']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'San Francisco taxes ground-floor storefronts left empty more than half the year (Prop D, 2020).'],
        ['Elsewhere', 'Vacant to Vibrant (San Francisco, 2023) gave pop-ups three months of free rent in empty downtown storefronts.'],
        ['Helps', 'Local boards must post agendas and take public comment (the Brown Act, 1953), a base for open neighborhood councils.'],
        ['County by county', 'Home businesses and new storefronts need local permits, and the rules vary.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'A public space registry, nearest-first claims and neighborhood business councils.']])) +

    sec2('3.5', 'Public jobs, kept', 'The city in transition', 'Nobody loses their public job. The work changes shape, and everyone gains the commons.',
      '<p><b>The promise to public workers:</b> no layoffs. You keep your job, your pay, your pension and your union, and you gain full access to the commons. Public-sector unions are friends, and so are private ones (' + go('3.9') + ').</p>' +
      '<h4>Work that grows</h4><p>Fire, 911, schools, parks, libraries, public health, public works and water.</p>' +
      '<h4>Work that changes shape</h4><ul class="gv-shift">' + [
        ['Policing survival crimes, jails, eviction court', 'Safety officers, crisis responders, restorative practitioners'],
        ['Code enforcement and permit desks', 'Home stewards and NMB business advisors'],
        ['Means-testing and eligibility checks', 'Housing matchmakers and commons coordinators'],
        ['Shelter administration', 'Commons staff'],
        ['Parking and citation enforcement', 'Litter crews and street care']].map(function (r) { return '<li><span>' + r[0] + '</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only">becomes</span><b>' + r[1] + '</b></li>'; }).join('') + '</ul>' +
      '<p><b>The honest worry:</b> commons land pays little or no property tax, so the city’s tax base shrinks. Its costs shrink too: shelters, jails, emergency room visits, eviction court. Pilots measure both, out loud.</p>' +
      law([['Not yet law', 'A no-layoffs transition plan that moves public workers into needs-meeting roles.']])) +

    sec2('3.6', 'Taxes, time and gifts', 'Paying for land', 'Money still matters, mostly for land. Time counts, and so do gifts of money.',
      '<p>The biggest cost of holding land is property tax. Lovernance keeps what taxes do well, lets more of it be given in time, and makes giving money easy for people who have more money than time.</p>' +
      ul([
        '<b>The commons owes no property tax,</b> so living there costs nothing in taxes. Without a job, you owe nothing, and being out of work stops being a crisis.',
        '<b>Local taxes in time.</b> Hours at a school, on a road crew or in a commons garden count toward property tax.',
        '<b>Money as a gift.</b> Someone with savings and little time can give money instead, to a neighbor or to the commons fund. A direct gift can go to one person, like a single mom out of work. A gift to the commons fund can’t be earmarked, but it’s tax-deductible.',
        '<b>Give and get.</b> “We’ll bring you food and elder care if you help cover your neighbor’s property tax this year.” People trade what they have more of, time or money.',
        '<b>Less needs money than it looks.</b> Food grows on underused land, and gifts of food, goods and time that would otherwise go to waste fill in the rest. The question is who has enough, not who can pay.',
        '<b>Shared costs shrink.</b> Testing, cleanup, insurance and repairs that each household pays alone today cost less when the community carries them together.',
        '<b>Drop-in shifts.</b> A shift at a neighborhood restaurant or garden counts as time given, with no commitment.',
        '<b>A student debt fund</b> at the community credit union, which also helps the commons and NMBs plan their money together (' + go('3.7') + ').',
        '<b>Estates and wills.</b> Give your home and keep living there for life, with a tax deduction now. Each home you give covers one household for good, your kids first (' + go('2.2') + '), so your family always has a home.',
        '<b>Everyday help isn’t counted.</b> Only bigger pledges get written down, for coordination (<a href="#lens-governance/2-3">2.3 Thanks, not rewards</a>).']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'California exempts low-income housing owned by nonprofits from property tax (the welfare exemption, R&amp;T Code §214), so commons homes can be fully exempt today.'],
        ['Federal', 'There’s no federal property tax, and someone with no income owes no federal income tax.'],
        ['Federal', 'Anyone can give up to $19,000 per person a year (2026) with no gift tax return; above that it’s a form, with no tax due until $15 million in lifetime gifts. Gifts to a nonprofit fund are deductible.'],
        ['Federal', 'Giving your home while keeping the right to live in it for life can count as a charitable gift (26 U.S.C. §170(f)(3)).'],
        ['In the way', 'Prop 19 (2020): children keep their parents’ low property tax only if they move into the home. Keeping “the family house” is getting expensive; a kin gift to the commons offers another way.'],
        ['On the ballot', 'Prop 40 (November 3, 2026): a one-time 5% tax on billionaires’ wealth for health care, food aid and schools. Lovernance gifts are the voluntary version.'],
        ['Not here yet', 'California has no property tax work-off. Massachusetts lets towns take a set amount off an older homeowner’s tax for volunteer work (Gen. Laws ch. 59 §5K); in Wilbraham it’s up to $1,000 a year.'],
        ['Federal', 'Trading goods or services as a deal can count as taxable income (IRS barter rules). Gifts and favors given freely, with no deal, are different.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'Paying local taxes in time, a pool for neighbors’ taxes, and a student debt fund at every community credit union.']])) +

    sec2('3.7', 'Old debts', 'Lifting what’s already owed', 'Debt people already carry is one of the hardest parts of the change. Forgiving it meets a need.',
      '<p>Student loans, medical bills and credit cards can keep someone working for a paycheck long after their home and food are covered. Lovernance treats forgiving a debt as meeting a need, the same as a meal or a room.</p>' +
      ul([
        '<b>Forgiving counts.</b> A lender, hospital or neighbor who forgives a debt is giving in give-and-get (' + go('2.3') + ').',
        '<b>Pennies on the dollar.</b> Old medical debt sells cheaply, so a little money can wipe out a lot of it. Gifts to the commons fund can buy debt to forgive it.',
        '<b>Lenders get something back.</b> The commons fund and community credit unions bank with lenders that forgive, and those lenders can step in as needs-meeting businesses (' + go('3.1') + ').',
        '<b>Public service clears student loans.</b> Ten years of full-time work for a nonprofit or a public employer can forgive what’s left of federal student loans. It meets an old-economy need, so it counts. A nonprofit that meets the four musts is an honorary NMB (' + go('3.1') + '), so nobody has to restructure and lose years toward forgiveness. Public school teachers already qualify, and a four-day week of eight-hour days still counts as full-time.',
        '<b>Low income, low payments.</b> With a home and food covered, income-based student loan payments can drop very low.',
        '<b>A student debt fund</b> at the community credit union helps pay down what’s left (' + go('3.6') + ').',
        '<b>A fresh start, when it’s safe.</b> Bankruptcy is a forgiveness the law already guarantees. Chapter 7 clears most credit card, medical and personal loan debt in a few months, and in California people keep a home up to a limit, a car, work tools and some savings. It stays on a credit report for up to 10 years, so it fits best once the commons reliably meets your needs, or when your credit is already past saving. It’s always your choice, never pushed.',
        '<b>Free legal help.</b> The commons fund pays for legal aid, so anyone who chooses bankruptcy files with a real lawyer. Low-income filers can ask the court to waive its fee.',
        '<b>File first, then give.</b> A gift to the commons made in the years before filing can be undone by the bankruptcy trustee. File first and keep what the law protects, then give it if you want to.',
        '<b>Some debts just fade.</b> Someone with no wages or property a creditor can take is “judgment-proof”: Social Security, SSI and most public benefits are protected, and in California most debts can’t be sued on after four years. Bankruptcy still gives a clean, final end, if that brings peace.',
        '<b>Debt never decides your place.</b> Homes and care go by need, never by credit score.']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'California hospitals must offer free or discounted care to patients with income up to 400% of the federal poverty level (AB 1020, 2021).'],
        ['Helps', 'Since 2025, medical debt can’t appear on California credit reports (SB 1061, 2024).'],
        ['Federal', 'Public Service Loan Forgiveness clears what’s left of federal Direct Loans after 120 qualifying payments while working full-time (at least 30 hours a week) for a government or 501(c)(3) nonprofit employer. A worker co-op or an LLC doesn’t qualify, one reason honorary NMBs exist.'],
        ['Federal', 'Forgiven debt can count as taxable income, with exceptions, including bankruptcy, Public Service Loan Forgiveness and debt forgiven when someone owes more than they own (26 U.S.C. §108).'],
        ['Federal', 'Chapter 7 bankruptcy discharges most unsecured debts, like credit cards and medical bills, usually within a few months (11 U.S.C. §727). Child support, most recent taxes, criminal fines and debts from fraud survive (§523). Low-income filers can ask to have the filing fee waived (28 U.S.C. §1930(f)).'],
        ['Helps', 'California exemptions let bankruptcy filers keep a home up to a limit, a car, work tools and some savings (Code Civ. Proc. §§703.140, 704.010–704.730).'],
        ['Helps', 'In California, most debts on a written contract can’t be sued on after four years (Code Civ. Proc. §337), and Social Security and SSI can’t be taken by creditors.'],
        ['In the way', 'Federal student loans are discharged in bankruptcy only for “undue hardship” (11 U.S.C. §523(a)(8)), a hard test, though a simpler federal process began in 2022.'],
        ['In the way', 'A bankruptcy trustee can undo gifts made within 2 years before filing (11 U.S.C. §548), or up to 4 years under California law (Civ. Code §3439.09), so gifts to the commons should come after filing.'],
        ['Elsewhere', 'Cook County, Illinois (2022), and other cities and counties have paid a nonprofit to buy and forgive residents’ medical debt for pennies on the dollar.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'Debt forgiveness counted as a needs-meeting gift, commons banking with lenders that forgive, a debt-buying fund, and commons-paid legal aid for bankruptcy.']])) +

    sec2('3.8', 'Less to throw away', 'Waste', 'Compost what can rot, use less plastic, and keep the rest away from people until it can be broken down safely.',
      '<p>A landfill is where needs went unmet: food nobody ate, things nobody could fix. Most of it can go back to the soil or back into use. Plastic is the hard part. Recycling it still means handling it, and plastic sheds tiny pieces and chemicals into food, water and bodies (<a href="#lens-toxins">Poisons</a>).</p>' +
      ul([
        '<b>Compost first.</b> Food scraps, yard waste and paper go to commons compost and worm farms, and back to the gardens.',
        '<b>Use less plastic.</b> NMBs choose glass, metal, paper and cloth, refill instead of rebuy, and skip single-use plastic wherever they can.',
        '<b>Fix and pass on.</b> Repair shops, thrift and the tool library keep things in use (' + go('3.3') + ').',
        '<b>A plastics NMB.</b> Its workers collect plastic, keep it sealed and out of homes and kitchens, and send it out safely for now, with protective gear and fair shares for work most people would rather not do.',
        '<b>Research on fungi and microbes</b> that can digest plastic, funded and open to all. It’s early: some break down certain plastics in a lab, and none work at scale yet.',
        '<b>Watch what goes out.</b> The commons keeps an eye on what still goes to the landfill, so it can shrink year by year.']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'Since 2022, California requires cities to collect food scraps and yard waste for composting, and big grocers to donate edible food instead of throwing it out (SB 1383, 2016).'],
        ['Helps', 'Makers of single-use plastic packaging must cut how much they sell by 25% by 2032 and pay for what happens to it afterward (SB 54, 2022).'],
        ['In the way', 'Only a small share of plastic is ever recycled, and recycled plastic can carry the same chemicals into new products.'],
        ['Elsewhere', 'In 2011, Yale students found a fungus in Ecuador (<i>Pestalotiopsis microspora</i>) that can break down one plastic, polyurethane, in the lab.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'A plastics NMB in every county, and public research on fungi and microbes that digest plastic.']])) +

    "<section class=\"gv-sec\" id=\"gv-3-9\"><div class=\"gv-sec-head\"><span class=\"gv-n\">3.9</span><div><h2>Unions</h2><p>Every union, public and private</p></div></div><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Unions won the weekend. Every union is invited in, public and private.</p><p>Unions are some of the oldest needs-meeting groups there are. They won the weekend, the eight-hour day and safer work. Worker co-ops are like unions that own the shop, and lovernance invites every union in.</p><ul><li><b>Public unions keep everything</b> they have, with no layoffs (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-5\">3.5</a>).</li><li><b>Private unions too.</b> Nurses, grocery and hotel workers, building trades, drivers, teachers at private schools. A transit union belongs in lovernance as much as a city department does.</li><li><b>Contracts stay.</b> Going all in as an NMB never undoes a union contract, and NMB workers can always organize.</li><li><b>Union co-ops.</b> The union bargains and trains, and the workers own. A home care co-op in the Bronx has been worker-owned and union for decades.</li><li><b>Apprenticeships train NMB crews.</b> Union training halls already teach electricians, plumbers and carpenters, and NMB builders can learn there (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-3\">3.3</a>).</li><li><b>A seat at the table.</b> Unions are invited to neighborhood business councils and commons decisions (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-4\">3.4</a>, <a href=\"#lens-governance/4-6\">4.6</a>), and union members can join the safety membership (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-2\">3.2</a>).</li><li><b>Strike funds are mutual aid.</b> On a picket line, the commons kitchen feeds everyone.</li><li><b>Pensions can sell to the program.</b> Union pension funds often own homes through big landlords. A sale over years gives them steady income (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-2\">2.2</a>).</li></ul><h4>California today</h4><ul class=\"gv-law\"><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Federal</span> The National Labor Relations Act (1935) protects most private-sector workers who organize and bargain together. It leaves out independent contractors and supervisors.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> California’s public workers bargain under state law: cities and counties under the Meyers-Milias-Brown Act (1968), schools under the Educational Employment Relations Act (1975).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> AB 288 (2025) lets the state labor board (PERB) hear private-sector cases when the federal board won’t or can’t. Court challenges are expected.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> The state approves apprenticeship programs, many run jointly by unions and employers (Division of Apprenticeship Standards).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Elsewhere</span> The United Steelworkers and Mondragon published a model for union co-ops in 2012. Cooperative Home Care Associates in the Bronx is worker-owned and its workers belong to SEIU 1199.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Not yet law</span> Union seats on neighborhood councils, fair-rate contracts at union scale, and the safety membership for every union member.</li></ul></section>" +

    "<section class=\"gv-sec\" id=\"gv-3-10\"><div class=\"gv-sec-head\"><span class=\"gv-n\">3.10</span><div><h2>Clean power</h2><p>Energy</p></div></div><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Peace begins at home: households move off gas one appliance at a time, and the makers are NMBs.</p><p>Burning gas and oil harms people who live near wells, refineries and gas stoves, and fights over oil reach far from home. Peace begins at home. A household can change over the way it replaces anything: when the old one wears out.</p><ul><li><b>One appliance at a time.</b> When the water heater, furnace or stove gives out, it’s replaced with a heat pump or induction. Nothing that works gets thrown out early (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-8\">3.8</a>).</li><li><b>Help switching.</b> Commons electricians, wiring upgrades and a portable induction burner to try first.</li><li><b>Makers, not just installers.</b> Businesses that make solar panels, batteries, heat pumps, induction stoves, wind turbines and wiring can all be NMBs, along with the people who repair and recycle them (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-1\">3.1</a>).</li><li><b>Sun on commons roofs.</b> Panels and batteries at the commons keep the lights and fridges on for the block in an outage (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-1\">2.1</a>).</li><li><b>Gas workers keep working.</b> Pipe fitters and utility crews move into electric and water work, with their unions (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-9\">3.9</a>).</li><li><b>Needing less is a success.</b> Good insulation, shade trees and shared laundries cut how much power anyone needs (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-8\">2.8</a>).</li></ul><h4>California today</h4><ul class=\"gv-law\"><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> California’s goal is 100% clean electricity by 2045 (SB 100, 2018).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> In the Bay Area, new water heaters made from 2027 and new furnaces made from 2029 can’t release nitrogen oxides, which in practice means heat pumps (Bay Area Air District rules 9-4 and 9-6).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> Community choice energy lets cities buy cleaner power for everyone, like CleanPowerSF (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3-3\">3.3</a>).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> Since 2023, new rooftop solar earns much less for power sent back to the grid (net billing).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Federal</span> The federal tax credit for home solar and batteries ended after 2025.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Not yet law</span> NMB status for clean energy makers, commons microgrids, and switching help for every household.</li></ul></section>" +

    '<p class="gv-src">Sources: AB 816, California Worker Cooperative Act (2015), Cal. Corp. Code §12243; 26 U.S.C. §§1381–1388; SB 1162 (2022); NYC Worker Cooperative Business Development Initiative (2014); Cal. Health &amp; Safety Code §113735 and after (California Retail Food Code); SMUD; CleanPowerSF; S.F. Proposition D (2020); Ralph M. Brown Act (1953); Cal. Corp. Code §12200; Cal. Rev. &amp; Tax. Code §214; IRS annual gift exclusion and lifetime exemption (2026); 26 U.S.C. §170(f)(3); Cal. Const. art. XIII A §2.1 (Prop 19); Proposition 40 (2026); Mass. Gen. Laws ch. 59 §5K; IRS Topic 420, bartering income; Transfarmation Project (2019); AB 1020 (2021); SB 1061 (2024); Public Service Loan Forgiveness, 20 U.S.C. §1087e(m); 26 U.S.C. §108; Cook County medical debt relief (2022); SB 1383 (2016); SB 54, Plastic Pollution Prevention and Packaging Producer Responsibility Act (2022); Russell et al., <i>Applied and Environmental Microbiology</i> (2011); National Labor Relations Act (1935); Meyers-Milias-Brown Act (1968); Educational Employment Relations Act (1975); AB 288 (2025); Cal. Division of Apprenticeship Standards; USW, Mondragon and OEOC, union co-op model (2012); Cooperative Home Care Associates; SB 100 (2018); Bay Area Air District Regulation 9, Rules 4 and 6 (2023); CPUC net billing tariff (2023); 26 U.S.C. §25D; S.F. Vacant to Vibrant (2023).</p>';

  /* Unit 4 · Care and safety (dashed ids gv-4-1 … gv-4-8) */
  var UNIT4 =
    '<section class="gv-sec"><p><b>The good parts of taxes and law stay:</b> 911, the fire department, schools, roads, clean water and medical care. What changes is who gets support first, who decides, and how much can be given in time instead of money.</p>' +
    '<p>Nobody has to leave real safety to try something new. These are the protections that carry everyone through the change.</p>' +
    sublist(U4SEC) + '</section>' +

    sec2('4.1', 'Least able first', 'Who goes first', 'When there isn’t enough yet, people with the fewest ways to meet their own needs go first.',
      ul([
        '<b>The first pilot:</b> the lowest-income schoolchildren and their families, arranged by school district, get permanent homes in the program.',
        '<b>Every new baby</b> is born into safety, with a commons place for life (' + go('4.2') + ').',
        '<b>Families with children</b> get first pick of private homes, and the match keeps kids in their school (' + go('2.2') + ').',
        '<b>Renters before landlords</b> for needs-meeting goods and jobs (' + go('2.4') + ').',
        '<b>Then outward:</b> disabled people, elders and anyone without a home, and from there, everyone.']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'The Local Control Funding Formula (2013) gives schools extra money for each low-income student, English learner and foster youth, so districts already know where to start.'],
        ['Federal', 'McKinney-Vento: students without stable housing can stay in their school of origin, with transportation.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'A district-by-district pilot that houses the lowest-income students’ families for good.']])) +

    sec2('4.2', 'Born into safety', 'Kids first', 'Every baby born from launch day on has a commons place for life.',
      '<p>Kids have needs they can’t meet alone, and they can’t vote. Instead of one more line to wait in, each new year of babies is covered from birth. It starts small and reaches everyone within a generation.</p>' +
      ul([
        '<b>Birth standing.</b> Every baby born after launch day gets a lifelong place in the commons, and new parents get a welcome kit.',
        '<b>Kids already here</b> go first by need: the lowest-income schoolchildren and their families, then outward (' + go('4.1') + ').',
        '<b>A pledge covers your kids now.</b> Promise your home to the program, and your kids get standing today (' + go('2.2') + ').',
        '<b>Schools as stations.</b> Teachers, aftercare and school babysitters are covered as needs-meeting work. Garden educators visit so kids learn to grow food, cook and mend.',
        '<b>Meet the animals.</b> Every school has a sanctuary to visit, so kids get to know cows, goats and hens (' + go('2.5') + ').',
        '<b>A voice for kids.</b> Teens vote on commons and school matters, and a kids’ council brings younger voices to commons meetings (' + go('4.6') + ').']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'Every baby born in California since July 2022 gets a starter college savings account (CalKIDS).'],
        ['Helps', 'California public schools offer free breakfast and lunch to every student (since 2022), and transitional kindergarten is open to every 4-year-old (since 2025).'],
        ['Elsewhere', 'Finland has given new parents a box of baby clothes and supplies since 1938.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'Birth standing in the commons, and schools staffed as needs-meeting stations.']])) +

    sec2('4.3', 'Health together', 'Prevention first', 'A shared health program for staying well, clinics close to home, and hospitals for the big things.',
      '<p>People who eat more produce, walk more and feel less stressed tend to need less medical care, so health care can become a smaller, gentler industry.</p>' +
      ul([
        '<b>Staying well:</b> yearly checkups and dental exams, nutrition, movement, mental health, and care at home, close to where people live.',
        '<b>Care without insurance in the middle.</b> Doctors, dentists and veterinarians can step off insurance, charge fair rates they choose, and be taken care of by the commons with a home, a space and the shared program.']) +
      '<h4>Three levels of care</h4>' + ul([
        '<b>A commons clinic</b> close to home: checkups, dental exams, therapy, and urgent care with X-rays, so most broken bones are set and cast without a hospital.',
        '<b>A surgery center</b> for planned operations like hernias, knees and cataracts: clean, accredited, and owned by the surgeons and staff who work there, as an NMB.',
        '<b>A hospital for the big things.</b> A car crash, a heart attack or cancer still goes to a hospital, through regular insurance or Medi-Cal. The program sits on top, never instead.']) +
      '<h4>Help with the old system, for now</h4>' + ul([
        '<b>Insurance and Medi-Cal carry the big bills</b> through the transition. A shared fund for dental, glasses, therapy and time to heal can grow as the commons does, but prevention comes first.',
        '<b>A bill fighter.</b> A community health worker helps people apply for a hospital’s free or discounted care, so a $100,000 bill often shrinks or disappears (' + go('3.7') + ').',
        '<b>Malpractice coverage is carried together,</b> so a doctor leaving a big system isn’t alone with that cost.']) +
      '<h4>Doctors who choose it</h4><p>Some doctors will choose a $400,000 salary in the old economy, and that’s their choice. Others went into medicine to help people, and would rather heal the sick in-house, at a commons clinic. A collective-owned home, a clinic space, safety for their family, and student loans forgiven through public service (' + go('3.7') + ') can be worth more than the pay they leave behind. Kids who grow up in safety and choose medicine will start out here (' + go('4.2') + '). In California, an employer can’t stop a doctor from leaving to practice nearby.</p>' +
      '<h4>Money that’s already there</h4>' + ul([
        '<b>Medi-Cal can pay for medically tailored meals,</b> so NMB kitchens can be paid to cook for people living with diabetes, heart failure or cancer.',
        '<b>Caregivers get paid.</b> In-Home Supportive Services pays people, often family, to care for elders and disabled people at home, including homes in the commons.',
        '<b>CalFresh</b> can work at commons farm stands and markets.',
        '<b>Community health workers</b> are paid by Medi-Cal for prevention, including the bill fighters.']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'California hospitals must offer free or discounted care to patients with income up to 400% of the federal poverty level (AB 1020, 2021).'],
        ['Helps', 'Medi-Cal’s Community Supports (CalAIM, 2022) can pay for medically tailored meals, help finding and keeping housing, respite for caregivers and more, depending on the county’s health plan.'],
        ['Helps', 'Medi-Cal has paid community health workers for prevention since 2022, and In-Home Supportive Services pays caregivers, family members included.'],
        ['Helps', 'Contracts that stop someone from working for a competitor are void in California (Bus. &amp; Prof. Code §16600), even when signed in another state (SB 699, 2023).'],
        ['Helps', 'Community health centers already charge on a sliding scale.'],
        ['Helps', 'Surgery with general anesthesia outside a hospital must happen in an accredited, licensed or Medicare-certified setting (Bus. &amp; Prof. Code §2216), which keeps a surgery center safe.'],
        ['In the way', 'Small employers can’t band together to buy one big group health plan through an association (SB 1375, 2018), with narrow exceptions.'],
        ['In the way', 'A clinic membership (direct primary care) is a gray area: if a monthly fee covers broad care, the state may treat it as a health plan that needs a license (Knox-Keene Act, 1975).'],
        ['In the way', 'CalAIM runs on a federal waiver, and federal Medicaid cuts passed in 2025 put some of this money at risk.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'A shared health program licensed as its own kind of plan, and surgery centers owned as NMBs with commons backing.']])) +

    sec2('4.4', 'Safer together', 'Insurance and disasters', 'Safety for every kind of land: commons, collective-owned homes and give-and-get.',
      ul([
        '<b>Disaster means a home tonight.</b> If a home burns or floods, the commons houses you right away while co-op builders rebuild. That promise is the real safety, more than any check.',
        '<b>Insurance owned by the people it covers.</b> A members’ mutual for all three kinds of land: no shareholders, and any surplus goes back into fire-hardening, not profit.',
        '<b>Harden the whole block together.</b> Neighbors, gardeners and time gifts do the work (ember-resistant vents, a clear 5-foot zone, irrigated gardens as green buffers), which earns home and community discounts.',
        '<b>Automatic payouts.</b> A neighborhood policy pays the local fund as soon as a fire or flood is measured, with no claims or adjusters.',
        '<b>Land safety is shared.</b> Testing for lead and other poisons is free, and cleanup is paid for together, because clean soil protects everyone downwind and downstream. You open your land to testing, and you get cleanup and peace of mind (<a href="#lens-toxins">Poisons</a>).',
        '<b>Like a forest.</b> California swings between wet and dry years, El Niño and La Niña, north and south. When one region is ailing, another gives water, food and homes, and gets them back when the weather turns. Give where you can, receive when you need: that’s interdependence.',
        '<b>A way out of the riskiest places.</b> Standing moves with you, so people can move somewhere safer without losing what they gave (' + go('2.2') + ').']) +
      law([
        ['In the way', 'Private insurers have pulled back, and the state’s last-resort FAIR Plan grew to about 697,000 policies by June 2026, up 157% since 2022. The Sustainable Insurance Strategy lets insurers raise rates if they write more in high-risk areas; whether that’s happening is disputed.'],
        ['Helps', '“Safer from Wildfires” (2022) requires insurers to give discounts for home and community fire-hardening, such as Firewise communities.'],
        ['Helps', 'Fremont (2024) became the first U.S. city to buy its own parametric flood policy, which pays automatically once flooding is measured. The California Earthquake Authority (1996) shows a publicly managed statewide pool can work.'],
        ['Helps', 'California already moves water between regions, and banks it underground in wet years for dry ones (the Kern Water Bank is one).'],
        ['Not yet law', 'A members’ mutual for lovernance land, and a statewide wildfire pool like the Earthquake Authority.']])) +

    sec2('4.5', 'Community safety', 'Help when you need it', 'Safety officers on call when people want them, and quiet ways to ask for help when someone can’t.',
      ul([
        '<b>911 stays,</b> and so does the fire department.',
        '<b>Town safety officers</b> are around when people need them: to keep someone safe, walk someone home, or calm a situation down.',
        '<b>Crisis responders</b> (medics and counselors) answer mental health calls.',
        '<b>Quiet ways to ask.</b> Some people can’t go to anyone openly, like someone being controlled at home or forced to work. Advocates check in gently, and a quiet signal at a diner, clinic or pharmacy can start help without anyone else knowing.',
        '<b>Early flags, met with care.</b> When someone’s behavior starts to worry the people around them, the first response is a circle of support: what do they need, and who can help? The behavior is paused, and the person stays part of the community.',
        '<b>Weapons stay out of shared spaces.</b> Commons halls, schools and stations are weapon-free by agreement. Self-defense is respected. In a crisis, weapons can be held for a while, the way a friend holds the car keys.',
        '<b>A small, trained team for high stakes,</b> called only when someone is in immediate danger, with a public report each time.',
        '<b>Repair after harm.</b> Restorative practitioners help everyone involved ask what happened, who was hurt, and what would make it right. Restorative and transformative justice keep people inside the community instead of othering them.',
        '<b>When harm keeps happening.</b> If someone keeps hurting a neighbor and repair isn’t working, they’re offered another home, so nobody ends up on the street. Moving someone without their agreement still takes a just cause and a court, as tenant law requires.',
        '<b>Less defense spending, more safety.</b> National defense stays, smaller, because communities whose needs are met need less of it.']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'San Francisco’s Street Crisis Response Team (2020) sends medics and counselors to mental health calls instead of police.'],
        ['Helps', 'A gun violence restraining order (2016) lets family and household members, and since 2020 employers, coworkers and teachers, ask a court to remove guns for a while from someone at risk (AB 61).'],
        ['Helps', 'Most tenancies past a year can end only for a just cause (AB 1482, 2019). Causes where the tenant is at fault include a nuisance and criminal activity on the property, and if the tenant doesn’t leave, only a court can order it.'],
        ['Helps', 'In domestic violence cases, a court can order the person causing harm to move out of a shared home (Family Code §6321).'],
        ['Helps', 'The CRISES Act (AB 2054, 2020) funds community-based emergency response pilots.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'Town safety officers in every commons, a quiet-signal network for people in coercion, weapon-free commons spaces, and a high-stakes team that reports publicly.']])) +

    sec2('4.6', 'Who decides', 'Lovernance', 'No one person at the top. Many people decide, close to home.',
      '<p>A whole city run by one mayor and a small board is a bottleneck, and big campaigns reward whoever can afford the most marketing. Lovernance spreads decisions out.</p>' +
      ul([
        '<b>Who votes:</b> people who live or work in a commons vote on it. Give-and-get neighbors vote on anything that touches their land.',
        '<b>Commons-run, not city-run.</b> The people most involved decide together, rather than waiting on one elected office.',
        '<b>Kids have a say.</b> Teens vote on commons and school matters, and a kids’ council brings younger voices (' + go('4.2') + ').',
        '<b>Pitch a policy for your neighborhood,</b> and your neighbors vote on it.',
        '<b>A project board.</b> Every local project shows three things side by side: how much it matters to people, how big a lift it is, and what it costs in taxes or time. Residents rank them, and the top ones get built.',
        '<b>Topic councils</b> for water, housing, food and the rest. Specialists can run without big campaigns: short written statements, public forums, low spending limits.',
        '<b>Lottery panels.</b> A random, representative group of interested residents studies a question, hears from experts and decides or recommends, the way a jury does.']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'Charter cities like San Francisco can change how they govern by a vote of residents (a charter amendment).'],
        ['Helps', 'Vallejo (2012) ran the first citywide participatory budget in the US: residents proposed and voted on how to spend part of the city budget.'],
        ['Helps', 'Voters in Berkeley (2016) and Oakland (2020) let 16- and 17-year-olds vote for school board.'],
        ['Elsewhere', 'East Belgium has had a permanent citizens’ council chosen by lottery since 2019.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'Neighborhood policy votes, topic councils and lottery panels with real decision power.']])) +

    sec2('4.7', 'Needs met, on your word', 'Unmet to met', 'No census. Say what you did, plainly, and it counts, including what you did before lovernance existed.',
      '<p>Lovernance doesn’t need an office counting everyone’s needs. It trusts people the way friends do: you say what you did, and it counts. Asking works the same way: say what you need, and neighbors and the commons step in.</p>' +
      ul([
        '<b>Say what changed.</b> One plain line: the need, who it was for, and what changed. “Installed a filter that serves twelve homes on our block; the tap tested high for lead before.” “My friend has lived in our spare room since 2024.”',
        '<b>Specific enough to point to.</b> A filter, a room, a garden bed, meals every Tuesday: something real, not a stack of forms. Nobody has to prove it unless there’s a real dispute, and then it’s talked through, restoratively (' + go('4.5') + ').',
        '<b>Early counts.</b> Already took in a friend you didn’t have to? Already feeding the block? It counts from the day you started, not the day lovernance arrived. Nobody should wait for credit to do the right thing, and nobody is penalized for doing it first.',
        '<b>Keeping it going counts too.</b> A room still offered and a filter still changed count as much as starting. This is how “who is newly cared for because of you?” gets answered (' + go('1.3') + ').',
        '<b>Asking is just as simple.</b> “I need ___,” said to the commons or a neighbor. No form and no proof of being poor for water, food, a bed or care (' + go('4.1') + ').',
        '<b>Never used against anyone.</b> What people share is for meeting needs, never for policing or citations (<a href="#lens-governance/5.2">5.2</a>).',
        '<b>Needing less is the goal.</b> “I don’t need this anymore” is a need met for good.',
        '<b>Seeing whether it works</b> doesn’t take a new survey. Fewer people sleeping outside, cleaner tap tests and less sent to the landfill show up in counts and reports that already exist.']) +
      law([
        ['Federal', 'Communities with federal homelessness funding count people without homes on one night in January (the point-in-time count): people in shelters every year, people outside at least every other year.'],
        ['Helps', 'Nothing stops neighbors from keeping their own record of needs met today.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'Needs met, on your word, counting toward standing, including needs met before the program began.']])) +

    sec2('4.8', 'Held at the end', 'Dying and death', 'Your wishes written down, care at home if you want it, and a gentle way back to the soil.',
      '<p>Death is part of every life, and of the land. Lovernance holds it the way it holds birth: close to home, with people who care, and without a bill that frightens a grieving family.</p>' +
      '<h4>Before</h4>' + ul([
        '<b>Your wishes, written down.</b> Community health workers help anyone fill out an advance directive and, when it’s time, a POLST form, so doctors know what care you want.',
        '<b>Hospice at home or in the commons,</b> paid by Medi-Cal or Medicare, with neighbors to sit with you and your family.',
        '<b>Choosing when.</b> California lets a terminally ill adult ask for medicine to end their life, with safeguards. The commons respects whatever each person chooses.',
        '<b>Your home goes where you say.</b> A pledge or kin gift settles it ahead of time (' + go('2.2') + ').',
        '<b>Estate planning is needs-meeting work.</b> Wills, trusts and written wishes for after you die, made with someone who knows how (' + go('3.3') + ').']) +
      '<h4>After</h4>' + ul([
        '<b>A home funeral is legal.</b> A family can care for their person at home, file the papers and arrange burial or cremation themselves, without a funeral home.',
        '<b>A quiet room in each commons,</b> kept cool, where a body can rest for a few days while people say goodbye.',
        '<b>Death doulas and funeral helpers are needs-meeting work</b> (' + go('3.3') + '): washing and dressing, paperwork, and sitting with the family.',
        '<b>Back to the soil.</b> Human composting turns a body into soil over a couple of months, and it’s legal in California starting in 2027. Water cremation and scattering ashes are other gentle options.',
        '<b>A burial ground, someday:</b> natural burial on protected land, with no vault and no embalming.',
        '<b>The coroner still comes</b> when a death is sudden, unexplained or unattended.',
        '<b>Grief is a need.</b> Circles, meals and time off for the people left behind.',
        '<b>Animals are mourned too.</b> Sanctuary animals who die are held with the same care (' + go('2.5') + ').']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'A person’s own written wishes, then a spouse or the closest family, decide what happens to a body (Health &amp; Safety Code §7100), and families can file the death certificate and get the permit themselves.'],
        ['Helps', 'Human composting (natural organic reduction) is legal starting January 1, 2027 (AB 351, 2022). Water cremation (alkaline hydrolysis) has been allowed since 2020 (AB 967, 2017).'],
        ['Helps', 'The End of Life Option Act (2016) lets terminally ill adults ask for aid in dying, extended through 2031 (SB 380, 2021). A POLST form carries care wishes into emergencies (Probate Code §4780).'],
        ['Helps', 'Ashes can be scattered where no local rule forbids it, with the landowner’s permission (Health &amp; Safety Code §7116).'],
        ['In the way', 'Burying a body outside a cemetery is a crime (Health &amp; Safety Code §7054), so a natural burial ground needs a cemetery license.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'A quiet room in every commons, death doulas as needs-meeting work, and natural burial grounds kept as protected land.']])) +

    '<p class="gv-src">Sources: Local Control Funding Formula (2013); McKinney-Vento Homeless Assistance Act, 42 U.S.C. §11431 et seq.; California Kids Investment and Development Savings Program (CalKIDS, 2022); Kela maternity package (Finland, 1938); SB 1375 (2018); Knox-Keene Health Care Service Plan Act of 1975; Medi-Cal community health worker benefit (2022); Cal. Code Regs. tit. 10 (Safer from Wildfires, 2022); California FAIR Plan data (June 2026); City of Fremont parametric flood policy (2024); California Earthquake Authority; Kern Water Bank; S.F. Street Crisis Response Team (2020); AB 2054, CRISES Act (2020); Gun violence restraining orders, Cal. Penal Code §18100 and after (2016), expanded by AB 61 (2019); S.F. Charter; Vallejo participatory budgeting (2012); Ostbelgien Citizens’ Council (2019); Berkeley Measure Y1 (2016); Oakland Measure QQ (2020); HUD Continuum of Care point-in-time count, 24 C.F.R. §578.7; Cal. Civ. Code §1798 (Information Practices Act, 1977).; AB 1020 (2021); Medi-Cal Community Supports (CalAIM, 2022); In-Home Supportive Services; Cal. Bus. &amp; Prof. Code §§2216, 16600; SB 699 (2023); Cal. Health &amp; Safety Code §§7054, 7100, 7116; AB 351 (2022); AB 967 (2017); End of Life Option Act (2016), SB 380 (2021); Cal. Prob. Code §4780; Cal. Civ. Code §1946.2 (AB 1482); Cal. Fam. Code §6321</p>';

  /* Unit 1 · Overview (dashed ids gv-1-1 … gv-1-6): what lovernance is, the evidence that there’s enough, ways in, the transition plan (without law, then with law), the legal to-do list, and how to ask for it. */
  var U1SEC = [['1.1', '“Lovernance” is interdependence'], ['1.2', 'There’s enough'], ['1.3', 'Ways in'], ['1.4', 'Transition plan'], ['1.5', 'What has to change'], ['1.6', 'Ask for it']];
